++# Agent Note: Client row config crosses the boot wire
++
++Status: implemented
++
++English | [中文](2026-08-30-client-row-config-channel.zh.md)
++
++> Scope: the composition config of a `dsh.client` row between the host Loader and the browser half. The [client plugin loading model](2026-07-23-client-plugin-loading-model.md) owns arrival and lifecycle, and the [client shell layering note](2026-08-15-client-shells-and-dynamic-packages.md) owns package placement and shared-module requests.
++
++## Problem
++
++A `dsh.client` package is one Loader row read by two halves: the host half receives `entry.options.config`, while the browser half was created from `window.__DSH_BOOT__`, whose `WebBootEntry` carried `id`/`url`/`rev`/`inject`/`immediately`/`external` and nothing else. A composition value a plugin wanted on both sides therefore had to be duplicated: the row config drove the host half, and the browser half read a built-in default or a settings document. A third-party front-door plugin reported the gap as the first of its open questions ([discussion #2648](https://github.com/deepseek-ai/deepseek-harness/discussions/2648)); row-config edits were also the one composition change the browser half could never observe.
++
++## Decision
++
++The boot graph carries the row config. `WebBootEntry.config` is the source Loader row's `config` as one JSON value, omitted when the row declares none; the client-modules node half projects `entry.options.config` at scan time and re-projects it when a live row's config changes, keeping the row's artifact revision. The browser manifest parser copies the field into `BootPluginRow.config`, and the boot kernel passes it to `loader.create({ name, config })`, so the browser half receives the same value through `apply(ctx, config)` as the host half.
++
++`assertJsonValue()` rejects a config that JSON would change or drop — functions, symbols, bigints, non-finite numbers, class instances, `undefined` members — and composition fails with the entry name and the JSON path. Silent divergence between the two halves is the failure this channel removes, so a value that cannot cross the wire is misconfiguration, not a reason to fall back.
++
++A config edit on a live row reaches the served graph (the next index render) but not an already-created fiber: refreshing a running entry's config remains an explicit `entry.update()` its owner performs. HMR keeps the row's config because `rebuilt()` rebuilds the row from the value already on it.
++
++## Alternatives considered
++
++**A settings namespace per plugin.** The [plugin-owned settings surface](2026-08-12-plugin-owned-settings-surface.md) is the sanctioned configuration channel for values a user edits in the browser, and it persists through the host document only on loopback connections. It is a different entry point: it adds a document, a namespace registration, and a settings card, and it cannot express a composition value that must be identical on both halves before the first render.
++
++**`dsh.client.external` plus a shared package.** Carrying the value as a module request shares code, not data; the value still needs a wire to the browser half.
++
++**Serializing the whole entry (`id`, `name`, `config`, `inject`, `disabled`).** The graph is a browser wire, not a copy of the Loader's config document: `disabled` rows are filtered out at composition, and the remaining fields keep their dedicated wire members.
++
++**Best-effort JSON that drops what does not serialize.** Dropping a function or an `undefined` member silently gives the browser half a different value than the host half, which is the defect this change removes.
++
++## Consequences
++
++Plugin authors declare a browser-half value once, in the composition row, and read it with `apply(ctx, config)`. The row config becomes browser-visible content: a dual-face row's config is served in the page, so a secret belongs in a credential or settings namespace, not in row config. The wire grows by one JSON value per configured row, and the graph's existing `<` escaping already covers plugin-controlled strings.
